    "description": "Cyrus II founded the Persian Empire conventionally known as Achaemenid and ruled until 530 BCE. His conquests, the royal center at Pasargadae, and the Babylonian inscription called the Cyrus Cylinder provide distinct perspectives on his reign and later reputation.",
    "tags": ["Cyrus II", "Achaemenid Empire", "Babylon", "Pasargadae", "Ancient history"],
    "status": "Deceased",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:33:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:33:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Cyrus the Great: Conquests, Evidence and Legacy | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Cyrus II through his conquests, Pasargadae, the Cyrus Cylinder, and the distinction between ancient evidence and modern commemoration."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "king-and-sources",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A founder whose early life is difficult to recover",
        "content": "Cyrus II, often called Cyrus the Great, was the founder of the Persian Empire conventionally known as Achaemenid. His reign is commonly dated approximately 559–530 BCE. His father was Cambyses I, and his royal ancestry was associated with Anshan, a kingdom in southwestern Iran. These connections anchor him in a regional political world before his conquests transformed the wider Near East. [s1]\n\nHis childhood is much harder to reconstruct. Greek authors transmitted conflicting stories involving royal dreams, threatened infants, and unexpected reversals of fortune. Herodotus himself knew multiple accounts of Cyrus’s origins. Such narratives reveal how an extraordinary rise was explained and remembered; their dramatic details should not be presented as a securely documented childhood. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conquest",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From regional kingship to empire",
        "content": "Cyrus’s defeat of Astyages, king of Media, around 550 BCE changed the regional balance of power. He subsequently overcame Lydia in western Anatolia, including its capital Sardis. These victories brought the Persian ruler into confrontation with the major powers farther west. In 539 BCE Persian forces defeated a Babylonian army at Opis before Cyrus entered Babylon. The distinction matters: the occupation of the city followed a military campaign, even though royal presentations emphasized a peaceful reception there. Egypt remained unconquered during his lifetime and fell to his son Cambyses later. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cylinder",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the Cyrus Cylinder establishes",
        "content": "The Cyrus Cylinder is a clay foundation inscription from Babylon, written in Akkadian using cuneiform, a system of wedge-shaped signs. Its account places Cyrus within Babylonian traditions of legitimate kingship. The god Marduk selects him, his predecessor Nabonidus is condemned, and Cyrus describes restored worship, the return of displaced communities, and work on Babylon’s defenses. These are claims in a royal building inscription, rather than an independent assessment of every imperial policy. [s3]\n\nIts modern reputation requires a separate explanation. The object became an important symbol during the Iranian monarchy’s anniversary celebrations in 1971. The Metropolitan Museum explicitly distinguishes that later role from the cylinder’s original purpose: it was not conceived as a guarantee of human rights in the modern sense. Respect for its historical importance does not require attributing a modern legal program to its author. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "pasargadae",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A royal landscape at Pasargadae",
        "content": "Pasargadae, in Fars in southern Iran, provides a material counterpart to the written evidence. UNESCO identifies it as Cyrus’s dynastic capital, with surviving palace remains, gardens, a fortified terrace, and his mausoleum. The ensemble shows early royal Achaemenid architecture combining different cultural influences. It is evidence of an imperial court’s built environment; it cannot by itself demonstrate how every conquered population experienced Persian rule. Read alongside the Babylonian inscription and later narratives, the site helps separate Cyrus’s historical setting from the ideals subsequently attached to his name. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reign-timeline",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected landmarks in Cyrus’s reign",
        "content": "```mermaid\ntimeline\n  title Cyrus II - selected landmarks\n  \"Approximately 559 BCE\" : \"Conventional beginning of reign\"\n  \"Around 550 BCE\" : \"Defeat of Astyages of Media\"\n  \"539 BCE\" : \"Conquest of Babylon\"\n  \"530 BCE\" : \"Death of Cyrus\"\n```",
        "summary": "The timeline distinguishes approximate early dates from the established year of Babylon’s conquest. It does not imply that every campaign can be dated precisely.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
